faq:
  - { q: "What does the Charities SORP 2026 ask about impact?", a: "The trustees' annual report must now show what difference the charity made, not only what it did. Tier 1 charities (income up to £500,000) must summarise their main achievements and should consider the difference made to beneficiaries and society. Tier 2 and 3 charities must explain the impact they are making and consider the long-term effect of their work. It applies to reporting periods beginning on or after 1 January 2026." }
  - { q: "How much does it cost?", a: "The Impact Health Check is £550 + VAT. The Impact Report Pack is fixed by your gross income: £1,650 + VAT up to £500,000 (3 consultant days), £2,750 + VAT from £500,000 to £1.5m (5 days) and £3,850 + VAT over £1.5m (7 days). The guide is £20." }
  - { q: "Will you write our trustees' report?", a: "We draft the achievements and impact section, with the evidence behind it, for your trustees to review and approve. Trustees remain responsible for the report. We do not prepare the rest of it." }
  - { q: "Does this cover our accounts or audit?", a: "No. Income recognition, leases, independent examination and audit belong with your finance team and examiner or auditor. We work on the narrative and the evidence, and we work alongside them." }
  - { q: "What if our impact evidence is thin?", a: "That is common, and it is a useful result. We say what you can show, what you observe and what you believe, and we set out what you cannot yet show. A report that says so is more credible than one that overclaims." }
  - { q: "Which SORP tier is our charity in?", a: "Tiers are set by gross income. Tier 1 is up to £500,000, Tier 2 is over £500,000 and up to £15 million, and Tier 3 is over £15 million. Each tier adds requirements to the one below. We confirm your tier in the first conversation." }
  - { q: "Do I need to buy the guide first?", a: "No. The guide is a low-cost way to understand the requirements. Many charities start with the Health Check instead, because it shows exactly where your current report and evidence fall short." }
